\begin{itemize}
    \item $h(2) = 10$
    \item $h(10) = 1$
    \item $h(1) = 2$
\end{itemize}
Since $h(x) = f^{-1}(x)$, we have:
\begin{itemize}
    \item $f^{-1}(2) = 10$
    \item $f^{-1}(10) = 1$
    \item $f^{-1}(1) = 2$
\end{itemize}
From the definition of the inverse function, if $f^{-1}(x)=y$, then $f(y) = x$. Therefore:
\begin{itemize}
    \item $f(10) = 2$
    \item $f(1) = 10$
    \item $f(2) = 1$
\end{itemize}
We are asked to find $f(f(10))$.
Since $f(10) = 2$, we have
$f(f(10)) = f(2) = 1$.

\boxed{1}

