Let the first line be $L_1$ and the second line be $L_2$. The direction vectors of $L_1$ and $L_2$ are $\vec{v}_1 = \begin{pmatrix} 3 \\ k \\ 7 \end{pmatrix}$ and $\vec{v}_2 = \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 4 \\ 7 \end{pmatrix}$, respectively.
The point on $L_1$ is $P_1 = \begin{pmatrix} -1 \\ -3 \\ -5 \end{pmatrix}$ and the point on $L_2$ is $P_2 = \begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ 4 \\ 6 \end{pmatrix}$.
For the lines to be coplanar, the vector $\vec{P_1 P_2} = P_2 - P_1 = \begin{pmatrix} 2 - (-1) \\ 4 - (-3) \\ 6 - (-5) \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 3 \\ 7 \\ 11 \end{pmatrix}$ must be coplanar with the direction vectors $\vec{v}_1$ and $\vec{v}_2$. This means that the scalar triple product $(\vec{P_1 P_2} \times \vec{v}_1) \cdot \vec{v}_2 = 0$.

We have:
\begin{align*} \vec{P_1 P_2} \times \vec{v}_1 &= \begin{pmatrix} 3 \\ 7 \\ 11 \end{pmatrix} \times \begin{pmatrix} 3 \\ k \\ 7 \end{pmatrix} \\ &= \begin{pmatrix} (7)(7) - (11)(k) \\ (11)(3) - (3)(7) \\ (3)(k) - (7)(3) \end{pmatrix} \\ &= \begin{pmatrix} 49 - 11k \\ 33 - 21 \\ 3k - 21 \end{pmatrix} \\ &= \begin{pmatrix} 49 - 11k \\ 12 \\ 3k - 21 \end{pmatrix} \end{align*}
Now, we take the dot product with $\vec{v}_2 = \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 4 \\ 7 \end{pmatrix}$:
\begin{align*} (\vec{P_1 P_2} \times \vec{v}_1) \cdot \vec{v}_2 &= \begin{pmatrix} 49 - 11k \\ 12 \\ 3k - 21 \end{pmatrix} \cdot \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 4 \\ 7 \end{pmatrix} \\ &= (49 - 11k)(1) + (12)(4) + (3k - 21)(7) \\ &= 49 - 11k + 48 + 21k - 147 \\ &= 49 + 48 - 147 - 11k + 21k \\ &= -50 + 10k \end{align*}
We set this equal to zero:
\begin{align*} -50 + 10k &= 0 \\ 10k &= 50 \\ k &= 5 \end{align*}
Therefore, $k = 5$.

\boxed{5}

