We are asked to divide $x^4-3x^3+4x^2+11x-9$ by $x^2-3x+2$ using synthetic division. Since we are dividing by a quadratic, synthetic division cannot be directly used. Instead, we will perform polynomial long division.

Divide $x^4-3x^3+4x^2+11x-9$ by $x^2-3x+2$.

\[
\begin{array}{c|ccccc}
\multicolumn{2}{r}{x^2} & & +2 \\
\cline{2-6}
x^2-3x+2 & x^4 & -3x^3 & +4x^2 & +11x & -9 \\
\multicolumn{2}{r}{x^4} & -3x^3 & +2x^2 \\
\cline{2-4}
\multicolumn{2}{r}{0} & 0 & 2x^2 & +11x & -9 \\
\multicolumn{2}{r}{} & & 2x^2 & -6x & +4 \\
\cline{4-6}
\multicolumn{2}{r}{} & & 0 & 17x & -13 \\
\end{array}
\]

Thus, we have $x^4-3x^3+4x^2+11x-9 = (x^2-3x+2)(x^2+2)+(17x-13)$.
Therefore, the remainder is $17x-13$.

\boxed{17x-13}

